.Fo OCSP_check_validity
.Fa "ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME *thisupd"
.Fa "ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME *nextupd"
.Fa "long sec"
.Fa "long maxsec"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OCSP_basic_verify
.Fa "OCSP_BASICRESP *bs"
.Fa "STACK_OF(X509) *certs"
.Fa "X509_STORE *st"
.Fa "unsigned long flags"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn OCSP_resp_find_status
searches
.Fa bs
for an OCSP response for
.Fa id .
If it is successful the fields of the response are returned in
.Em *status ,
.Em *reason ,
.Em *revtime ,
.Em *thisupd
and
.Em *nextupd .
The
.Em *status
value will be one of
.Sy V_OCSP_CERTSTATUS_GOOD ,
.Sy V_OCSP_CERTSTATUS_REVOKED
or
.Sy V_OCSP_CERTSTATUS_UNKNOWN .
The
.Em *reason
and
.Em *revtime
fields are only set if the status is
.Sy V_OCSP_CERTSTATUS_REVOKED .
If set the
.Em *reason
field will be set to the revocation reason which will be one of
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_NOSTATUS ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_UNSPECIFIED ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_KEYCOMPROMISE ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_CACOMPROMISE ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_AFFILIATIONCHANGED ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_SUPERSEDED ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_CESSATIONOFOPERATION ,
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_CERTIFICATEHOLD
or
.Sy OCSP_REVOKED_STATUS_REMOVEFROMCRL .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_count
returns the number of
.Vt OCSP_SINGLERESP
structures in
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0
returns the
.Vt OCSP_SINGLERESP
structure in
.Fa bs
corresponding to index
.Fa idx ,
where
.Fa idx
runs from 0 to OCSP_resp_count(bs) - 1.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_find
searches
.Fa bs
for
.Fa id
and returns the index of the first matching entry after
.Fa last
or starting from the beginning if
.Fa last
is -1.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_single_get0_status
extracts the fields of
.Fa single
in
.Em *reason ,
.Em *revtime ,
.Em *thisupd
and
.Em *nextupd .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_produced_at
extracts the
.Sy producedAt
field from the single response
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_signature
returns the signature from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_tbs_sigalg
returns the
.Sy signatureAlgorithm
from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_respdata
returns the
.Sy tbsResponseData
from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Xr OCSP_resp_get0_certs 3
returns any certificates included in
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_signer
attempts to retrieve the certificate that directly signed
.Fa bs .
The OCSP protocol does not require that this certificate is included in
the
.Fa certs
field of the response, so additional certificates can be supplied via
the
.Fa extra_certs
if the certificates that may have signed the response are known via some
out-of-band mechanism.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_id
gets the responder id of
.Fa bs .
If the responder ID is a name then <*pname> is set to the name and
.Em *pid
is set to NULL. If the responder ID is by key ID then
.Em *pid
is set to the key ID and
.Em *pname
is set to NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get1_id
is the same as
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_id
but leaves ownership of
.Em *pid
and
.Em *pname
with the caller, who is responsible for freeing them unless the function
returns 0.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_check_validity
checks the validity of its
.Fa thisupd
and
.Fa nextupd
arguments, which will be typically obtained from
.Fn OCSP_resp_find_status
or
.Fn OCSP_single_get0_status .
If
.Fa sec
is nonzero it indicates how many seconds leeway should be allowed in the
check.
If
.Fa maxsec
is positive it indicates the maximum age of
.Fa thisupd
in seconds.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_basic_verify
checks that the basic response message
.Fa bs
is correctly signed and that the signer certificate can be validated.
It takes
.Fa st
as the trusted store and
.Fa certs
as a set of untrusted intermediate certificates.
The function first tries to find the signer certificate of the response
in
.Fa certs .
It then searches the certificates the responder may have included in
.Fa bs
unless
.Fa flags
contains
.Sy OCSP_NOINTERN .
It fails if the signer certificate cannot be found.
Next, unless
.Fa flags
contains
.Sy OCSP_NOSIGS ,
the function checks the signature of
.Fa bs
and fails on error.
Then the function already returns success if
.Fa flags
contains
.Sy OCSP_NOVERIFY
or if the signer certificate was found in
.Fa certs
and
.Fa flags
contains
.Sy OCSP_TRUSTOTHER .
Otherwise the function continues by validating the signer certificate.
If
.Fa flags
contains
.Sy OCSP_PARTIAL_CHAIN
it takes intermediate CA certificates in
.Fa st
as trust anchors.
For more details, see the description of
.Sy X509_V_FLAG_PARTIAL_CHAIN
in
.Xr X509_VERIFY_PARAM_set_flags 3 .
If
.Fa flags
contains
.Sy OCSP_NOCHAIN
it ignores all certificates in
.Fa certs
and in
.Fa bs ,
else it takes them as untrusted intermediate CA certificates and uses
them for constructing the validation path for the signer certificate.
Certificate revocation status checks using CRLs is disabled during path
validation if the signer certificate contains the
.Sy id-pkix-ocsp-no-check
extension.
After successful path validation the function returns success if the
.Sy OCSP_NOCHECKS
flag is set.
Otherwise it verifies that the signer certificate meets the OCSP issuer
criteria including potential delegation.
If this does not succeed and the
.Sy OCSP_NOEXPLICIT
flag is not set the function checks for explicit trust for OCSP signing
in the root CA certificate.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OCSP_resp_find_status
returns 1 if
.Fa id
is found in
.Fa bs
and 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_count
returns the total number of
.Vt OCSP_SINGLERESP
fields in
.Fa bs
or -1 on error.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0
returns a pointer to an
.Vt OCSP_SINGLERESP
structure or NULL on error, such as
.Fa idx
being out of range.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_find
returns the index of
.Fa id
in
.Fa bs
(which may be 0) or -1 on error, such as when
.Fa id
was not found.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_single_get0_status
returns the status of
.Fa single
or -1 if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_produced_at
returns the
.Sy producedAt
field from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_signature
returns the signature from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_tbs_sigalg
returns the
.Sy signatureAlgorithm
field from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_respdata
returns the
.Sy tbsResponseData
field from
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Xr OCSP_resp_get0_certs 3
returns any certificates included in
.Fa bs .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_signer
returns 1 if the signing certificate was located, or 0 if not found or
on error.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_resp_get0_id
and
.Fn OCSP_resp_get1_id
return 1 on success, 0 on failure.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_check_validity
returns 1 if
.Fa thisupd
and
.Fa nextupd
are valid time values and the current time +
.Fa sec
is not before
.Fa thisupd
and, if
.Fa maxsec
>= 0, the current time -
.Fa maxsec
is not past
.Fa nextupd .
Otherwise it returns 0 to indicate an error.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_basic_verify
returns 1 on success, 0 on verification not successful, or -1 on a fatal
error such as malloc failure.
.Sh NOTES
Applications will typically call
.Fn OCSP_resp_find_status
using the certificate ID of interest and then check its validity using
.Fn OCSP_check_validity .
They can then take appropriate action based on the status of the
certificate.
.Pp
An OCSP response for a certificate contains
.Sy thisUpdate
and
.Sy nextUpdate
fields.
Normally the current time should be between these two values.
To account for clock skew the
.Fa maxsec
field can be set to nonzero in
.Fn OCSP_check_validity .
Some responders do not set the
.Sy nextUpdate
field, this would otherwise mean an ancient response would be considered
valid: the
.Fa maxsec
parameter to
.Fn OCSP_check_validity
can be used to limit the permitted age of responses.
.Pp
The values written to
.Em *revtime ,
.Em *thisupd
and
.Em *nextupd
by
.Fn OCSP_resp_find_status
and
.Fn OCSP_single_get0_status
are internal pointers which MUST NOT be freed up by the calling
application.
Any or all of these parameters can be set to NULL if their value is not
required.
